# MindFlow — repository line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Without it, how a file looks on disk depends on each developer's Git
# installation. `core.autocrlf=true` is the Git for Windows installer default
# and arrives in the SYSTEM config, so a Windows clone silently gets CRLF
# working files while the blobs stay LF, and a macOS or Linux clone gets LF.
# That made line endings a property of the machine rather than of the project,
# and it broke real things: source-text tooling anchored on "\n" stopped
# matching, and validators that rewrote their output in LF marked CRLF working
# files as modified even when nothing about the content had changed.
#
# This file makes the policy explicit and portable: the same checkout on every
# machine, whatever `core.autocrlf` happens to say.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `text=auto` lets Git decide text vs binary by content, so a new format is
# handled sensibly without a rule being written for it first.
# `eol=lf` pins the working-tree ending for everything Git decided is text.
# Together: LF in the index, LF on disk, on every platform. `core.autocrlf` no
# longer participates for any governed file.
#
# LF is the canonical form here because this is a Node / Next.js repository:
# the toolchain, the .mjs validation harnesses and the JSON evidence they read
# are all LF-native, and the index is already 100% LF (680/680 text blobs), so
# this pins the state the repository is already in rather than changing it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git's own heuristic already classifies every one of these correctly today.
# Declaring them anyway removes the heuristic from the path: a .glb whose
# header happens to look textual, or a small .ico, can never be line-ending
# converted. `binary` is Git's macro for `-text -diff` — no conversion, and no
# attempt to render a textual diff.
#
# Only formats actually present in the repository are listed.
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.glb   binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows batch scripts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are none today. This rule is here because their failure mode is silent
# and specific: cmd.exe can mis-execute a .bat/.cmd that has LF-only endings —
# labels and multi-line blocks are the usual casualties — and the default above
# would hand exactly that to anyone who adds one. Two lines now beats a
# confusing bug later.
#
# Deliberately NOT extended to .ps1: PowerShell reads LF scripts correctly, and
# the mojibake seen in this repository's history was a UTF-8 *encoding* issue,
# not a line-ending one. CRLF would not have fixed it. See ENCODING FOLLOW-UP
# in docs/archive/mindflow-repository-eol-policy-05/.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
